# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## {{lb|en|chemistry}} {{synonym of|en|sublimate}}
### To [[heat#Verb|heat]] (a [[substance#Noun|substance]]) in a [[container]] so as to [[convert#Verb|convert]] it into a [[gas#Noun|gas]] which then [[condense#Verb|condenses]] in [[solid#Adjective|solid]] [[form#Noun|form]] on [[cool#Adjective|cooler]] [[part#Noun|parts]] of the container; {{lb|en|generally}} to [[change#Verb|change]] (a solid substance) into a gas without [[break down|breaking down]] or [[pass#Verb|passing]] through the [[liquid#Adjective|liquid]] [[state#Noun|state]] by heating it [[gently]].
###* {{RQ:Jonson Alchemist|scene=v|sig=&#91;D3&#93;|page=42|passage={{smallcaps|Sub[tle]}}. {{...}} VVho are you? / {{smallcaps|Ana[nias]}}. A ''faithfull Brother'', if it pleaſe you. / {{smallcaps|Sub}}. VVhat's that? / A ''Lullianiſt''? a ''Ripley''? ''Filius artis''? / Can you '''''ſublime''''', and ''dulcefie''?}}
###* {{RQ:Grew Plants|chapter=II|chaptername=What may be Observed of Minerals|page=246|passage=''The aſhes either of Pit-Coal, or Sea-Coal, make no Efferveſcence vvith Alkalies or Acids.'' VVhence the ſaline Principle is altogether volatile, and '''ſublimed''' avvay by the fire.}}
### {{lb|en|archaic}} To [[obtain]] or [[purify]] (a substance) in this [[manner#Noun|manner]].
###* {{quote-journal|en|title=III. ''The Natural History of Niter, or, a Philosophical Discourse of the Nature, Generation, Place, and Artificial Extraction of Niter, with Its Vertues and Uses'', by William Clarke. London, 1670. in 8°. [book review]|journal=[[w:Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society|Philosophical Transactions: Giving Some Accompt of the Present Undertakings, Studies and Labours of the Ingenious in Many Considerable Parts of the World]]|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed by}} T. N. for John Martyn{{nb...|at the Bell, a little without Temple-Bar}}, printer to the {{w|Royal Society}}|year='''1670''' July 28 (Gregorian calendar)|volume=V|issue=61|page=2010|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=nxTe6gprrLsC&pg=PA2010|oclc=630046584|passage=[I]t [niter, or sodium carbonate] Calcineth, '''Sublimeth''' and Diſſolveth ''Minerals'' and ''Metals''.}}
## {{lb|en|by extension|figurative}} To raise (someone or an [[intangible#Adjective|intangible]] [[thing]]) to a state of (especially [[moral#Adjective|moral]] or [[spiritual#Adjective|spiritual]]) [[excellence]]; to [[exalt]].
##: {{synonyms|en|sublimate<q:archaic>}}
##* {{RQ:Sidney Astrophel and Stella|year=1591|sonnet=77|page=23|passage=Thoſe vvords vvhich doe '''ſublime''' the quinteſſence of bliſſe, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shirley Schoole of Complement|page=31|passage=Miſtris ''Medulla'', the Sunne of honour ſhine upon your hopes, till it '''ſublime''' you to a Ladiſhip: I vvill attend you preſently.}}
##* {{RQ:Milton Soveraigne Salve|page=35|passage=[W]e may finde {{...}} that confirmation in grace, I say, by which free will is transfigured and '''sublimed''' into a state divine; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Taylor Great Exemplar|part=2|chapter=Ad. Sect. 10. Considerations Touching the Vocation of Five Disciples, and of the First Miracle of Jesus Done at Cana in Galilee.|page=8|passage=[B]y his &#91;{{w|Jesus}}'s&#93; preſence he alſo hallovved marriage, and made it honourable, not onely in a civill account, and the rites of Heraldry, but in a ſpirituall ſenſe, he having nevv '''ſublim'd''' it by making it a Sacramentall repreſentment of the union of Chriſt and his Spouſe the Church.}}
##* {{RQ:Taylor Clerus Domini|paragraph=11|page=17|passage=An extraordinary miniſtery needs an extraordinary and a miraculous gift; that is a miraculous ''calling'' and ''vocation'' and ''deſignation'' by the holy Ghoſt; but an ordinary gift cannot '''ſublime''' an ordinary perſon to a ſupernaturall imployment; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Du Fresnoy Dryden Painting|page=7|passage=[T]hat Art being ſtrengthned by the knovvledge of things, may at length paſs into Nature by ſlovv degrees; and ſo in proceſs of time may be '''ſublim'd''' into a pure Genius vvhich is capable of chooſing judiciouſly vvhat is true; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Ferdinand|volume=II|chapter=His Return to England, and Midnight Pilgrimage to Monimia's Tomb|page=239|passage=Monimia hears not my complaints; her ſoul, '''ſublimed''' far, far above all ſublunary cares, enjoys that felicity, of vvhich ſhe was debarred on earth.}}
##* {{RQ:Burke Revolution in France|pages=273–274|pageref=273|passage=[H]e, the œconomiſt, diſpoſer, and ſhepherd of his ovvn kindred, '''ſubliming''' himſelf into an airy metaphyſician, vvas reſolved to knovv nothing of his flocks, but as men in general.}}
##* {{RQ:Southey Roderick|canto=III|page=45|passage=Call it not / Revenge; thus sanctified and thus '''sublimed''', / 'Tis duty, 'tis devotion.}}
##* {{RQ:Hazlitt Dramatic Literature|page=57|passage=This character may be considered as a personification of the pride of will and eagerness of curiosity, '''sublimed''' beyond the reach of fear and remorse.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Robert Montgomery (poet)|Robert Montgomery]]|chapter=Book VI|title=The Messiah.{{nb...|A Poem, in Six Books.}}|location=London|publisher=John Turrill,{{nb...|250, Regent-Street.}}|year=1832|page=243|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=gxwkAAAAMAAJ&pg=RA3-PA243|oclc=1143826143|passage=That beauty, which the hues of thought create / By thee enchanted,—slakes the mental fire / That parches us within: and yearning dreams, / And aspirations high as immortality, / Thy power '''sublimeth''' with mysterious aid.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=III|chapter=XII|page=193|passage=His very selfishness therefore is '''sublimed''' into public spirit: and this public spirit is stimulated to fierce enthusiasm by sympathy, by the desire of applause, and by the dread of infamy.}}
##* {{RQ:Hardy Trumpet-Major|volume=III|chapter=A Discovery Turns the Scale|page=94|passage={{...}} Bob's countenance was '''sublimed''' by his recent interview, like that of a priest just come from the ''penetralia'' of the temple.}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete}}
### To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (someone or something) to [[ascend]]; to [[raise#Verb|raise]] (someone or something) to a [[high#Adjective|high]] [[position#Noun|position]].
###* {{RQ:Massinger City-Madam|page=45|passage=I am '''ſublim'd'''! groſſe earth / Supports me not. I vvalk on ayr!}}
###* {{RQ:Denham Poems|edition=4th|poem=Cato Major|page=34|passage=But thou (dear Vine) forbid'ſt me to be long, / Although thy Trunk be neither large, nor ſtrong, / Nor can thy Head (not helpt) it ſelf '''ſublime''', / Yet like a Serpent, a tall Tree can climb, {{...}}}}
###* {{quote-journal|en|author=&#91;{{w|Edwin Percy Whipple}}&#93;|title=Editor’s Table|journal=[[w:Harper's Magazine|Harper’s New Monthly Magazine]]|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Harper (publisher)|Harper & Brothers]],{{nb...|publishers, 327 to 335 Pearl Street, Franklin Square.}}|month=July|year=1857|volume=XV|issue=LXXXVI|page=265|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/harpersnew15harper/page/265/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=924884025|passage=Formerly, to attack a community intrenched in laws, customs, institutions, and beliefs, required dauntless courage—a soul '''sublimed''' by an idea above the region of vanity and conceit—a character resolutely facing responsibilities it clearly realized, and especially a penetrating vision into the spirit and heart of the objects assailed.}}
### To cause ([[juice#Noun|juice]] or [[sap#Noun|sap]]) to rise in a [[plant#Noun|plant]].
###* {{RQ:Parkinson Theatrum Botanicum|chapter=''Camphora.'' Camfire.|page=1575|passage=[Camfire, ''i.e.'', camphor] ſeemeth plainely to be ſo made by art, being caſt as it vvere or '''ſublimed''' into broad round pans or diſhes and little above the thickneſſe of ones thumbe, {{...}}}}
### Especially of the [[sun#Noun|sun]]: to heat (something) and cause [[vapor#Noun|vapours]], etc., to rise from it.
###* {{RQ:Pope Essay on Criticism|page=24|passage=Some the ''French'' VVriters, ſome our ''ovvn'' deſpiſe; / The ''Ancients'' only, or the ''Moderns'' prize: / {{...}} / Meanly they ſeek the Bleſſing to confine, / And force ''that Sun'' but on a ''Part'' to Shine; / VVhich not alone the ''Southern VVit'' '''ſublimes''', / But ripens Spirits in cold ''Northern Climes''; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Kingsley At Last|volume=I|pages=181–182|pageref=181|passage=[O]n the swamps of the Caroni the malarious fog hung motionless in long straight lines, waiting for the first blaze of sunrise to '''sublime''' it and its invisible poisons into the upper air, where it would be swept off, harmless, by the trade-wind which rushed along half a mile above our heads.}}
### To purify (someone) from a [[bad#Adjective|bad]] [[influence#Noun|influence]] or from [[sin#Noun|sin]].
### To raise (someone) to a high [[office#Noun|office]] or [[status]]; to [[dignify]], to exalt.
###: {{synonyms|en|sublimate}}
###* {{RQ:Jonson Alchemist|scene=i|sig=B2|page=13|passage=Thou Vermine, haue I tane thee, out of dung, / {{...}} / '''''Sublim'd''''' thee, and ''exalted'' thee, and ''fix'd'' thee / I'the ''third region'', the ''high ſtate of grace''?}}
### To raise (a [[physical#Adjective|physical]] thing) to a state of excellence; to [[improve]].
###: {{synonyms|en|sublimate}}
###* {{RQ:Taylor Real Presence|section=V|para=10|page=476|passage=[I]t [bread used for communion] is made 'sacramental and eucharistical,' and so it is '''sublimed''' to become the body of Christ. But it is natural food still, {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=V|lines=482–484|page=141|passage=[F]lours and thir fruit / Mans nouriſhment, by gradual ſcale '''ſublim'd''' / To vital Spirits aſpire, {{...}}|year=1873}}
###* {{RQ:Cheyne Regimen|page=36|section=27|passage=[T]hat ''ſpiritual'' Subſtance vvas ''analogous'' to ''Matter'' infinitely rarefied, refin'd or '''''ſublim'd''''': Then, in the ''Geometrical'' Manner of conſidering ſuch reſpective Qualities and their Subject, ''Body'' infinitely rarefied, refin'd and '''ſublim'd''', vvould at laſt terminat in limited ''Space'' or ''Expanſion''; {{...}}}}
###* {{RQ:Byron Don Juan|canto=II|stanza=CLXXX|page=209|passage=Ring for your valet—bid him quickly bring / Some hock and soda-water, then you'll know / {{...}} / For not the blest sherbet, '''sublimed''' with snow, / {{...}} / After long travel, ennui, love, or slaughter, / Vie with that draught of hock and soda-water.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}}
## {{lb|en|chemistry}} {{synonym of|en|sublimate}}
### Of a substance: to change from a [[solid#Noun|solid]] into a gas without passing through the liquid state, with or without being heated.
###* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:John Uri Lloyd|J[ohn] U[ri] Lloyd]]|chapter=Elixirs|title=Elixirs: Their History, Formulæ, and Methods of Preparation{{nb...|Including Practical Processes for Making the Popular Elixirs of the Present Day, and those which have been Officinal in the Old Pharmacopœias: Together with a Résumé of Unofficinal Elixirs from the Days of Paracelsus}}|series=Pharmaceutical Preparations|edition=2nd|location=Cincinnati, Oh.|publisher=Robert Clarke & Company|year=1883|page=9|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=NGg3AAAAMAAJ&pg=PA9|oclc=224302115|passage=Then diſtil it, and you ſhall have a Philoſophical ☿ [mercury], and what '''Sublimeth''' is the Sulphur, which keep apart.}}
### Of a substance: to change from a gas into a solid without passing through the liquid state.
## {{lb|en|by extension|figurative}} To [[become]] higher in [[quality#Noun|quality]] or status; to improve.

