#* {{RQ:Dryden Aeneis|book=6|lines=740–741 and 747–750|page=384|passage=The Heroe, looking on the left, eſpy'd / A lofty Tovv'r, and ſtrong on ev'ry ſide / {{...}} / Vain is the force of Man, and Heav'ns as van, / To cruſh the Pillars vvhich the Pile ſuſtain. / '''Sublime''' on theſe a Tovv'r of Steel is rear'd; / And dire ''[[w:Tisiphone|Tiſiphone]]'' these keeps the VVard.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}}
## Of an [[aspect#Noun|aspect]] of [[art#Noun|art]] or [[nature#Noun|nature]]: [[cause#Verb|causing]] [[awe#Noun|awe]] or [[deep#Adjective|deep]] [[respect#Noun|respect]] due to its [[beauty#Noun|beauty]] or [[magnificence]]; [[awe-inspiring]], [[impressive]].
##: {{ux|en|'''sublime''' scenery}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|author=John Munro|title=A Trip to Venus|chapter=VI|chapterurl={{fullurl:s:en:A_Trip_to_Venus/Chapter_VI}}|passage=We had entered the clouds. For half-an-hour we were muffled in a cold, damp mist, and total darkness, and had begun to think of going indoors when, all at once, the car burst into the pure and starlit region of the upper air. A cry of joyous admiration escaped from us all. The spectacle before us was indeed '''sublime'''.}}
## Of [[flight#Noun|flight]]: [[ascending#Adjective|ascending]], [[soaring#Adjective|soaring]].
## Of an [[idea]] or other [[thing]]: [[require|requiring]] great [[intellectual#Adjective|intellectual]] [[effort#Noun|effort]] to [[appreciate]] or [[understand]]; very [[elevated#Adjective|elevated]], [[refined#Adjective|refined]], or [[subtle]].
## Of [[language]], [[style#Noun|style]], or [[writing#Noun|writing]]: [[express#Verb|expressing]] [[opinion]]s in a [[grand#Adjective|grand]] [[way#Noun|way]].
## Of a [[person#Noun|person]] or their [[action#Noun|actions]] or [[quality#Noun|qualities]]: [[intellectually]], [[morally]], or [[spiritually]] [[superior#Adjective|superior]].
##: {{ux|en|a '''sublime''' deed}}
##* {{RQ:Longfellow Voices|page=13|passage=Know how '''sublime''' a thing it is, / To suffer and be strong.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Matthew Bridges|chapter=Song of the Seraphs &#91;{{w|Crown Him with Many Crowns}}&#93;|title=The Passion of Jesus, a Collection of Original Pieces Corresponding with the Five Sorrowful Mysteries in the Rosary of Our Blessed Lady|location=London; Dublin|publisher=Richardson and Son,{{nb...|172, Fleet Street; 9, Capel St., Dublin; and Derby.}}|year='''1851''' (date written)|year_published=1852|section=stanza 5|page=64|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=OLjLLQNX1EsC&pg=PA64|oclc=506522199|passage=Crown Him the Lord of Years! / The Potentate of Time,— / Creator of the rolling spheres, / Ineffably '''sublime'''!}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Douglas Coupland|chapter=Adventure without Risk is Disneyland|title=w:Generation X: Tales for an Accelerated Culture|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:St. Martin's Press|St. Martin’s Press]]|year=1991|page=159|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/generationx00doug/page/159/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-312-11814-3|passage=You know, when I first met you, Claire, I thought that here might finally be a chance for me to be a class-act for once. To develop something '''sublime''' about myself. Well ''fuck '''sublime''''', Claire. I don't want dainty little moments of insight. I want ''everything'' and I want it ''now''.}}
## Of an [[office#Noun|office]] or [[status]]: very high; [[exalted#Adjective|exalted]]; also, {{non-gloss definition|[[use#Verb|used]] as an [[honorific#Noun|honorific]] (often [[capitalize]]d as '''Sublime''') to [[refer#Verb|refer]] to someone of high office or status, especially the [[Ottoman#Adjective|Ottoman]] [[sultan]]; or to things [[associated#Adjective|associated]] with such a person.}}
##: {{ux|en|the [[Sublime Porte|'''Sublime''' Porte]]}}
## Of a thing: [[consummate#Adjective|consummate]], [[perfect#Adjective|perfect]]; {{lb|en|informal|loosely}} [[excellent#Adjective|excellent]], [[marvellous]], [[wonderful#Adjective|wonderful]].
##* {{RQ:Prior Poems|poem=Sherlock|page=37|passage=Thy even Thoughts vvith ſo much Plainneſs flovv, / Their Senſe untutor'd Infancy may knovv; / Yet to ſuch height is all that Plainneſs vvrought, / VVit may admire, and letter'd Pride be taught: / Eaſie in VVords thy Style, in Senſe '''ſublime''', / On its bleſt Steps each Age and Sex may riſe, / 'Tis like the Ladder in the Patriarch's Dream, / Its foot on Earth, its height beyond the Skies.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|author=Richard Klein|chapter=Cigarettes are Sublime|title=Cigarettes are Sublime|location=Durham, N.C.; London|publisher=w:Duke University Press|year=1993|pages=62–63|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/cigarettesaresub00klei/page/62/mode/1up|isbn=978-0-8223-1401-1|passage=Cigarettes are poison and they taste bad; they are not exactly beautiful, they are exactly '''sublime'''. The difference, to use the terms in which [[w:Immanuel Kant|[Immanuel] Kant]] makes the distinction, means that smoking cigarettes gives rise to forms of aesthetic pleasure painfully at odds with the affect arising from the contemplation in tranquillity, say, of a well-wrought urn.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|poetic|archaic}} Of a person: [[dignified#Adjective|dignified]], [[majestic]], [[noble#Adjective|noble]].
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1842|author=w:Thomas De Quincey|title=Cicero|journal=Blackwood's Edinburgh Magazine|passage=the '''sublime''' Julian leader}}
