====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable|archaic}}
## Something which is [[sublime#Adjective|sublime]]; a [[sublimity]].
##* {{RQ:Shadwell True Widow|page=6|passage=''Car''[''los'']. VVhat is your opinion of the Play? / ''Yo''[''ung''] ''Mag''[''got'']. {{...}} There are a great many '''ſublimes''' that are very Poetical.}}
##* {{RQ:Burnet Varillas|part=Defence|page=8|passage=[S]ince there are tvvo ſorts of '''''Sublimes''''', the one of ''Nonſence'', and the other of ''Eloquence'', I vvill not take upon me to judge to vvhich of theſe this belongs.}}
##* {{RQ:Warburton Prodigies|pages=78–79|pageref=78|passage=Novv, vvhat a fine Opportunity vvas here of introducing his Story, in all the Blaze and Terror of anxious and diſordered Nature? VVith vvhat a '''Sublime''' might that Flaſh of Lightning have been brought in, to grace the approaching Ruin, {{...}}}}
## ''In the form'' '''the sublime of''': the [[high#Adjective|highest]] [[degree]]; the [[acme]], the [[height]].
##* {{RQ:Byron Beppo|stanza=LXX|page=36|passage=No solemn, antique gentleman of rhyme, / Who having angled all his life for fame, / And getting but a nibble at a time, / Still fussily keeps fishing on, the same / Small "Triton of the minnows," the '''sublime''' / Of mediocrity, the furious tame, {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} ''Chiefly preceded by'' '''[[the#Article|the]]'''.
## An [[aspect#Noun|aspect]] of [[art#Noun|art]] or [[nature#Noun|nature]] which [[cause#Verb|causes]] [[awe#Noun|awe]] or [[deep#Adjective|deep]] [[respect#Noun|respect]] due to its [[beauty#Noun|beauty]] or [[magnificence]]; hence, the great beauty or magnificence of a [[place#Noun|place]], a [[thing]], etc.
##* {{RQ:Burke Sublime and Beautiful|sectionnumber=XI|section=Why Visual Objects of Great Dimensions are Sublime|page=132|passage=[T]he vvhole capacity of the eye, vibrating in all its parts muſt approach near to the nature of vvhat cauſes pain, and conſequently muſt produce an idea of the '''ſublime'''. Or if vve take it, that one point only of an object is diſtinguiſhable at oince; the matter vvill amount nearly to the ſame thing, or rather it vvill make the origin of the '''ſublime''' from greatneſs of dimenſion yet clearer.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Harriet Beecher Stowe|H[arriet] Beecher Stowe]]|title=Sketches from the Note Book of an Old Gentleman. No. I.—The Old Meeting House.|editors=[[w:Sarah Josepha Hale|Sarah J[osepha] Hale]]; [[w:Lydia Sigourney|Lydia H[untley] Sigourney]]; [[w:Louis Antoine Godey|Louis A[ntoine] Godey]]|journal=[[w:Godey's Lady's Book|Godey’s Lady’s Book, and Ladies’ American Magazine]]|location=Philadelphia, Pa.|publisher=Louis A[ntoine] Godey,{{nb...|211 Chestnut Street.}}|month=August|year=1840|volume=XX|page=61|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=DtYRAAAAYAAJ&pg=RA1-PA61|column=1|oclc=1041857864|passage=[Our old meeting house's] double row of windows, of which I knew the number by heart, its doors with great wooden quirls over them, its belfry projecting out that the east end, its steeple and bell, all inspired as much sense of the '''sublime''' in me as Stratsburg Cathedral itself, and the inside was not a whit less imposing.}}
## A [[style#Noun|style]] of [[language]] or [[writing#Noun|writing]] which [[express#Verb|expresses]] [[opinion]]s in a [[grand#Adjective|grand]] [[way#Noun|way]].
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|edition=3rd|page=100|passage=And, after, feigning pique at what she call'd / The raillery, or grotesque, or false '''sublime'''— / Like one that wishes at a dance to change / The music—clapt her hands and cried for war, / Or some grand fight to kill and make an end: {{...}}}}
## That which is [[intellectually]], [[morally]], or [[spiritually]] [[superior#Adjective|superior]] in [[human#Adjective|human]] [[life#Noun|life]] or [[human nature]].
##* {{RQ:Paine Age of Reason|part=2|page=22|passage=The '''ſublime''' and the ridiculous are often ſo nearly related, that it is difficult to claſs them ſeparately. One ſtep above the '''ſublime''', makes the ridiculous; and one ſtep above the ridiculous, makes the '''ſublime''' again.}}
##* {{RQ:Poe Usher|page=145|passage=There was an iciness, a sinking, a sickening of the heart—an unredeemed dreariness of thought which no goading of the imagination could torture into aught of the '''sublime'''. What was it—I paused to think—what was it that so unnerved me in the contemplation of the House of Usher?}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=[[w:Willa Cather|Willa Sibert Cather]]|title=[[w:Eric Hermannson's Soul|Eric Hermannson’s Soul]]|journal=[[w:Cosmopolitan (magazine)|The Cosmopolitan: A Monthly Illustrated Magazine]]|location=Irvington-on-the-Hudson, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:John Brisben Walker|J[ohn] B[risben] Walker]]; from the Cosmopolitan Press|month=April|year=1900|volume=XXVIII|issue=6|page=633|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_cosmopolitan_1900-04_28_6/page/633/mode/1up|column=2|oclc=228687900|passage=For Asa Skinner was a man possessed of a belief, of that sentiment of the '''sublime''' before which all inequalities are leveled, that transport of conviction which seems superior to all laws of condition, under which debauchees have become martyrs; which made a tinker an artist and a camel-driver the founder of an empire.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|archaic}} The [[quality#Noun|quality]] or [[state#Noun|state]] of being [[sublime#Adjective|sublime]]; [[sublimeness]], sublimity.
#* {{RQ:Swift Tale of a Tub|chapter=The Preface|page=22|passage=[W]hatever VVord or Sentence is Printed in a different Character, ſhall be judged to contain ſomething extraordinary either of ''VVit'' or '''''Sublime'''''.}}

